---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You'll spend most weeks doing the same three things in a new domain:
    read a real case where finishing turned out to be the hard part,
    manipulate a small interactive model of that domain's specific cost
    curve, and argue, in writing, why that domain's version of the problem is
    or isn't like the others we've already covered. Two weeks break the
    pattern on purpose, one where the thesis stops applying and you have to
    say why, and a synthesis week where you stop collecting cases and start
    building your own framework. You walk out with a "last-mile audit", a
    real, unfinished thing you know personally, analysed using the framework
    the course built with you.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    This is for anyone who has ever shipped something they were secretly
    embarrassed to call finished, and wants a better vocabulary for why. No
    prerequisites in any specific domain, weeks cover software, law,
    aerospace, and hospitality with equal seriousness, but you do need to be
    comfortable reading a real case study and arguing with it in writing, not
    just summarising it. If you want a course that stays comfortably in one
    field, this isn't it, if you want one argument tested against eleven
    different kinds of evidence, it is.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve weekly labs — what you make, in what group size, each week.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Schedule" href="/schedule/">
      <p>Every lecture, lab and assessment due date, in one timetable.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
